  "kicker": "FACT",
  "topic": "Programming",
  "title": "On Unix, a file's name is not part of the file",
  "body": "An inode stores a Unix file's metadata and data pointers but not its name; directories are tables mapping names to inode numbers. That split is why hard links work: two names, one inode, one body of data, freed only when the last name and last open handle both go away.",
